{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-222465","model_call":32,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3474"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3474"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1358104787#8_1527220901","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3474","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1358104787#8_1527220901\" , \"end_char\": 8384.000000 , \"headings\": \"Public-order crime<cover>Public-order crime Contents England and Wales Crimes without apparent victims The hidden crime factor Decriminalization of public order crimes Arguments in favor of decriminalization Arguments against decriminalization Specific examples Prostitution Drugs See also Notes References External links \" , \"segment\": \"Information about birth control is no longer considered obscene (see the U.S. case law examples ). Within the context of a discussion (Feinberg: 1984) on whether governments should regulate public morals </cover>in the interest of the public good, Meier & Geis (1997) identify which social problems might be deemed appropriate for legal intervention and the extent to which the criminal law should enforce moral positions which may lack societal consensus. This reflects a more fundamental problem of legal consistency. People have the right to engage in some self-destructive activities. For all its carcinogenic qualities, tobacco is not a prohibited substance. Similarly, the excessive consumption of alcohol can have severe physical consequences, but it is not a crime to consume it. This ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1613399743#4_2833752520","rank":1,"relevance":2}]},"time":8},"model_calls":32,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
