{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-222465","model_call":33,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3474"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3474"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1358104787#6_1527216273","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3474","rank":3,"snippet":"underage sex, and pornography; and the offences involving substance abuse which may or may not involve some element of public disorder or danger to the public as in driving while intoxicated. Since 1965, however, societal views have changed greatly, for example, prostitution, often considered a victimless crime, is classified by some countries as a form of exploitation of women—such views are held in Sweden, Norway, and Iceland, where it is illegal to pay for sex, but not to be a prostitute (the client commits a crime, but not the prostitute), see <cover>Prostitution in Sweden . When deciding whether harm to innocent individuals should be prohibited, the moral and political beliefs held by those in power interact and inform the decisions to create or repeal crimes without apparent victims. These decisions change over time as moral standards change. For example, Margaret Sanger who founded the first birth control clinic in New York City was accused of distributing obscene material and violating public morals.\" , \"start_char\": 5347.000000 , \"title\": \"Public-order crime - </cover>Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-order_crime\" }"},"time":9},"model_calls":33,"document_judgments":24,"relevance_3":10,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
